        "2": {
            "name": "text_block",
            "id": 311,
            "position_x": 862,
            "position_y": 45,
            "width": 768,
            "height": 776,
            "visibility": "show_all",
            "draggable": true,
            "presentation_state": "show_all",
            "properties": {
                "0": {
                    "name": "block",
                    "value": "Gives every value that is in either list - each value once, sorted from smallest to largest (or in alphabetical order, for words).\n\nUse it to combine two selections into one, each value once: the joints used by either hand, the notes of two chords together, the channels flagged by either test.\n\nThese treat the lists as sets: how many times a value occurs, and where, does not matter. Numbers and words both work.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.union1d\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe first list, A (any shape is flattened). Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe second list, B, held: send it first.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nin either:\nThe result, sorted, each value once. It may be empty.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA is 1 2 3 4 and B is 3 4 5: in either, 1 2 3 4 5. A metro re-sends A twice a second;\nedit either list and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.intersect1d and np.setdiff1d are the other set operations. np.isin marks which elements of a list are in a set. np.unique gives the values of one list, each once.",
                    "value_type": "str"
                },
                "1": {
                    "name": "lock",
                    "value": true,
                    "value_type": "bool"
                },
                "2": {
                    "name": "width",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "3": {
                    "name": "height",
                    "value": 760,
                    "value_type": "int"
                },
                "4": {
                    "name": "text_size",
                    "value": "30",
                    "value_type": "str"
                }
            },
            "text": "Gives every value that is in either list - each value once, sorted from smallest to largest (or in alphabetical order, for words).\n\nUse it to combine two selections into one, each value once: the joints used by either hand, the notes of two chords together, the channels flagged by either test.\n\nThese treat the lists as sets: how many times a value occurs, and where, does not matter. Numbers and words both work.\n\nSYNTAX:\nnp.union1d\n\nINPUTS and PARAMETERS:\n\nin:\nThe first list, A (any shape is flattened). Every input is compared and sent - the only inlet that makes the node send.\n\nin 2:\nThe second list, B, held: send it first.\n\nOUTPUTS:\n\nin either:\nThe result, sorted, each value once. It may be empty.\n\nREADING THE DEMO:\nA is 1 2 3 4 and B is 3 4 5: in either, 1 2 3 4 5. A metro re-sends A twice a second;\nedit either list and click it.\n\nRELATED:\nnp.intersect1d and np.setdiff1d are the other set operations. np.isin marks which elements of a list are in a set. np.unique gives the values of one list, each once."
        },
